      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Seventy-nine patients with angiographically documented coronary artery disease were studied with exercise ECG and Holter ECG for ischaemic ST segment changes. Fifty-four patients (68.3%) had ischaemia on exercise, 48 patients (62.0%) had ischaemic ST segment depression during Holter monitoring. Twenty-four (30%) of the patients with a positive exercise test and 30 (61%) with a positive Holter ECG were asymptomatic during the test, 83.7% of the total count of 456 episodes of spontaneous ischemia during Holter monitoring were silent. Forty-four patients (55.7%) had ischaemic ECG changes during exercise and Holter ECG, 20 patients (25.3%) were negative on both tests. Ten (12.7%) had only a positive exercise test and five (6.3%) only a positive Holter ECG. The sensitivity of Holter monitoring for the detection of ischaemia in patients with coronary artery disease is comparable to the sensitivity of the exercise ECG."},
